-- title:
--   Unramifiedness of ∞-type cusps of X₀(4M') over X₀(M')
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ together with the hypothesis $q = 2$, and a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$; let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$. Inside the Laurent series field $L((\mathsf{q}))$ consider the intermediate field $K$ over $L$ assumed equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 (q ^ 2 * M'))`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101), that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((\mathsf{q}))$ generated by the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ of the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((\mathsf{q}))$ generated by the quotients $\iota(p_f)/\iota(p_g)$ of $q$-expansions of integral-$q$-expansion modular forms of equal weight for $\Gamma_0(q^2M')$. Let $j \in K$ be the element whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) $= \mathsf{q}^{-1} \cdot j_{\mathrm{num}}$, and $J_2 \in K$ the element whose series is the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jqN (q ^ 2)`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L194), the Laurent series obtained from `jq` by multiplying all exponents by $q^2$. Let $K_0$ be the corresponding intermediate field for $\Gamma_0(M')$, assumed contained in $K$. Let $w$ be a place of $K$ over $L$, i.e. a proper valuation subring of $K$ containing the image of $L$ and which is a principal ideal ring, and write $\operatorname{ord}_w$ for minus the logarithm of the associated $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation. Assume $\operatorname{ord}_w j < 0$ and $\operatorname{ord}_w J_2 = q^2 \cdot \operatorname{ord}_w j$. Then the ramification index of $w$ along the inclusion $K_0 \hookrightarrow K$, defined as the infimum of the positive integers of the form $\operatorname{ord}_w f$ for nonzero $f \in K_0$, equals $1$.
--
--   This is the assertion that a cusp of $X_0(q^2M')$ of $\infty$-type, characterised valuation-theoretically by $\operatorname{ord}_w j(q^2\tau) = q^2 \operatorname{ord}_w j(\tau)$ at a pole of $j$, is unramified over the corresponding place of the level-$M'$ curve $X_0(M')$; it is the $q = 2$ case of the statement proved for $q \ge 5$ elsewhere in the development. It feeds the subsequent analysis of such cusps in terms of the $j$-chart at infinity and nonunits of the Gauss valuation, within the study of the covering $X_0(q^2M') \to X_0(M')$.
